INT. ST. AMBROSE BAKERY - OVEN TUNNEL - NIGHT

A brick vault, black with a century of smoke. Iron doors, cooling racks, the slow smell of yesterday's bread. At the far end, a hole in the wall where the old coal chute used to be, just wide enough for a crouching man.

ODILE LAFARGE-MENDOZA, 40s, in a baker's apron over a black jumpsuit, kneels at the wall with a chisel and a mallet, coaxing a brick loose with a baker's patience. MARCELLUS TREMAINE, 60s, flour on his forearms, stands guard at the tunnel mouth, ear cocked toward the street. TOBIAS GRÜNEWALD, 20s, the apprentice, hugs a lantern and a tray of rolls for cover.

ODILE
Marcellus. Time.

MARCELLUS
Eleven past.

ODILE
Then we have until half.

TOBIAS
What happens at half?

MARCELLUS
The delivery truck.

TOBIAS
I thought it came at four.

MARCELLUS
It does. But the driver likes to sit and smoke.

ODILE
Which gives us twenty minutes.

TOBIAS
To do what?

ODILE
To go through a wall.

Tobias swallows. Sets down the tray. The tunnel is hot and the lantern is trembling. Odile pries the brick free. It falls with a soft cough. Beyond: dark, a draft of cold air, a faint metallic gleam.

MARCELLUS
That's it.

ODILE
That's it.

TOBIAS
What is?

MARCELLUS
The bank vault.

TOBIAS
The bank is on the other side of the street.

ODILE
And the bakery is on this side. And the tunnel crosses under.

TOBIAS
You never said it was a bank.

MARCELLUS
We said it was bread.

TOBIAS
This is not bread!

ODILE
Hush.

She puts a finger to her lips. They listen. The tunnel breathes. Somewhere above them, a car passes, tires hissing on wet cobbles.

ODILE (CONT'D)
Tobias. You've worked here three years.

TOBIAS
Yes.

ODILE
Have we ever been late with a loaf?

TOBIAS
No.

ODILE
Have we ever been short?

TOBIAS
No.

ODILE
So do you trust me?

TOBIAS
...Yes.

ODILE
Then hold the lantern.

He holds it. His hand stops shaking. She crawls into the hole. Marcellus passes her the mallet, then the chisel, then a long, thin steel probe. She disappears into the dark.

A scrape. A tap. A silence. A long, long silence.

TOBIAS
(whispering)
What's she doing?

MARCELLUS
Listening.

TOBIAS
To what?

MARCELLUS
To the lock.

TOBIAS
Locks don't talk.

MARCELLUS
Everything talks, lad. You just have to be quiet enough.

A click. A heavy mechanical sigh. Odile's voice drifts out of the hole, muffled and amused.

ODILE (O.S.)
Gentlemen. We're in.

Marcellus and Tobias exchange a look. Tobias laughs, a cracked, delighted laugh, and covers his mouth with a flour-white hand. Marcellus claps him on the back. The lantern swings, throwing gold across the old brick.

MARCELLUS
Twenty minutes, Odile.

ODILE (O.S.)
Eighteen.

TOBIAS
What do we do?

MARCELLUS
We bake.
